        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How a Surface Cleaner Actually Cleans
          </h2>

          <p>
            Under the round deck sits a horizontal bar, sometimes called a spray bar, mounted on a swivel. The bar has a nozzle at each end, angled slightly so the water jets push the bar around, the same way a lawn sprinkler spins. As it rotates, the nozzles sweep a full circle at a constant distance from the ground. You push the deck along, and the circular sweeps overlap into an even, clean path.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two things make this better than a wand. First, standoff distance stays constant. With a wand, small changes in height and angle create the light and dark stripes everyone recognizes on freshly washed concrete. Second, the nozzle hits each spot from many directions as it passes, which cleans texture more thoroughly than a single straight-on stripe. The deck also contains spray, so far less water and grime bounce back onto you, the house and the car.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed of rotation is the key performance factor. A bar that spins fast lays down overlapping sweeps closely, and you can walk at a steady pace without leaving swirl marks. A bar that spins slowly leaves arcs and requires you to crawl. Rotation depends on water flow through the nozzles, which is why matching the attachment to your machine's flow rate matters more than its maximum pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surface cleaners are rinse tools as much as cleaning tools. They pair best with a chemical pre-treatment: apply a driveway cleaner or degreaser, let it dwell, then run the surface cleaner over the slab to lift and flush it. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-driveway-cleaner/">best driveway cleaners</a> covers which chemistry suits which stains, and for oil specifically, see the <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Sizing: Match the Deck to Your PSI and GPM
          </h2>

          <p>
            Diameter should follow your machine's flow, not your driveway's size. A larger deck has a longer bar and needs more water to spin it at useful speed. Put too large a cleaner on a low-flow machine and it rotates sluggishly, leaving swirl marks and forcing you to move slowly. Put too small a cleaner on a high-flow machine and you waste capacity, though it will still clean well.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
